<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hohenstaufen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1079–<a href="/years/1268">1268</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Staufer, House of Hohenstaufen, Hohenstaufen dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hohenstaufen, or Staufer, were a Swabian dynasty named after their castle on the Hohenstaufen mountain. Dukes of Swabia from 1079, they became German kings with Conrad III in 1138 and emperors with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, Henry VI and Frederick II, who also ruled the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Their long conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> ended in their ruin, and the last of the line, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conradin">Conradin</a>, was executed in 1268.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen.jpg" alt="Hohenstaufen" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ssolbergj · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Or_three_leopards_sable.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origin of the family remains unclear. Staufer counts are mentioned in a document of Emperor Otto III of 987 as descendants of counts of the Riesgau in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>. A count Frederick, who died about 1075, appears as progenitor in a pedigree drawn up in 1153 at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>. His son Frederick of Büren married Hildegard of Egisheim, a niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-ix">Pope Leo IX</a>, and their son Frederick I was appointed Duke of Swabia by the Salian king Henry IV in 1079, when he was also engaged to the king's daughter Agnes.</p><p>The name Hohenstaufen was first used in the 14th century to tell the high conical hill in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Jura</a> from the village below, and historians applied it to the dynasty in the 19th century. German historians now often prefer Staufer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When the last Salian emperor, Henry V, died without heirs in 1125, Duke Frederick II and his brother Conrad, grandsons of Henry IV through their mother Agnes, lost the election to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Lothair of Supplinburg</a>. A civil war ended with Frederick's submission in 1134. After Lothair died, Conrad was elected king as Conrad III in 1137 and took part in the failed Second Crusade. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, king from 1152 and emperor after 1155, struggled to restore royal power against the dukes and spent decades fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombard-league">Lombard League</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a> denied him complete victory. He died in 1190 on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>.</p><p>His son Henry VI married Constance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and gained the Kingdom of Sicily, a source of vast wealth, but died in 1197. A civil war followed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a> and the Welf Otto IV, and Philip was murdered in 1208. Frederick II, raised in Sicily, was elected king in 1215 and crowned emperor in 1220. He spent little time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-naples-federico-ii">University of Naples</a> in 1224 and issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutions-of-melfi">Constitutions of Melfi</a> in 1231. Popes excommunicated him in 1227 and 1239 and a church council condemned him as a heretic in 1245. After Conrad IV died in 1254 the Great Interregnum followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Staufer line ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>, promised the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> by the popes, defeated Manfred at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-benevento">Battle of Benevento</a> on 26 February 1266. Manfred was killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conradin">Conradin</a>'s campaign to retake Sicily ended with his defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tagliacozzo">Battle of Tagliacozzo</a> in 1268, after which Charles had him publicly executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. The Kyffhäuser Monument, inaugurated in 1896, commemorates Frederick I, and the Hohenstaufen arms were adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> in 1954. Frederick II's Castel del Monte in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, built in the 1240s, was designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1996.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hohenstaufen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1191–1192 Count Frederick III of Zollern, a follower of the <b>Staufer</b> emperors, acquired the office of Burgrave of Nuremberg through his marriage to Sophia of Raabs, ruling there as Frederick I. After his death, around 1200–1204, his sons Conrad and Frederick at first held the inheritance together…</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the thirteenth century the kings were often absent, as Frederick II and his <b>Hohenstaufen</b> heirs ruled from Europe, and the baronial Ibelin family led resistance to imperial officials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Naples emerged from the break-up of the Kingdom of Sicily, created by the Norman Roger II in 1130 and later ruled by the <b>Hohenstaufen</b> emperors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom’s decline began with the war over the Norman inheritance and the <b>Hohenstaufen</b>–papal conflict, but the decisive break was the Vespers of 1282.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry VI of the <b>Hohenstaufen dynasty</b>, son of Frederick Barbarossa and King of Germany since 1169, was crowned Holy Roman Emperor.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-15">15 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Using a hidden reserve, Charles I of Anjou defeated Conradin, the sixteen-year-old last heir of the <b>Hohenstaufen</b>, who had come to reclaim the Kingdom of Sicily.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-23">23 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
